# Advanced Color Systems for Professional Design Software

For a professional desktop-first design application—such as a label editor, vector graphics tool, image editor, presentation designer, or document layout application—the best approach is not to choose a collection of attractive colors. It is to build a semantic, multi-layered color system that controls the entire application consistently.

For a TypeScript application built with React, Vite or Next.js, Zustand and CSS variables, the architecture should separate the application interface, document canvas, selection and editing overlays, semantic status colors, data visualizations, and exported artwork.

The goal is to achieve the consistency, precision and usability expected from mature creative applications such as Adobe, Affinity, Figma and professional IDEs, while allowing users to customize the appearance without compromising the document being designed.

## 1. Recommended professional color palettes

These are proposed production-oriented palettes, not official proprietary palettes from the named companies. They are starting points to validate against your application's actual components, display conditions and accessibility requirements.

