  };
}

export interface ThemeDefinition {
  id: ThemeId;
  label: string;
  colorScheme: "light" | "dark";
  colors: SemanticColorTokens;
}

```

In production, extend this schema for typography, spacing, density, shadows, motion, chart palettes and editor overlays. Validate that each theme supplies all required tokens.

## 7. Zustand, persistence and dynamic theming

Zustand should manage the user's selected theme, customization preferences and other application-level theme settings—not replace CSS as the styling engine.

A suitable state model might include:

```

type ThemeMode = "system" | "light" | "dark";

interface ThemePreferences {
  mode: ThemeMode;
  themeId: ThemeId;
  highContrast: boolean;
  useCustomAccent: boolean;
  customAccent?: string;
}

interface ThemeState {
  preferences: ThemePreferences;
  setMode: (mode: ThemeMode) => void;
  setTheme: (themeId: ThemeId) => void;
  setHighContrast: (enabled: boolean) => void;
  setCustomAccent: (color: string) => void;
}

```

Implementation requirements:

- Persist user preferences across application restarts.
- Resolve system mode through `prefers-color-scheme` when `mode === "system"`.
- Apply theme variables at a predictable root, such as the application shell.
- Avoid changing the document's own colors when the theme changes.
- Validate custom accent colors against the required foreground and interaction contrasts.
- Restore a valid default theme if saved preferences reference a theme that no longer exists.
- Ensure newly opened windows and plugin panels inherit the correct theme.
- Avoid persisting transient states such as hover, focus, drag or pressed states.
- Prevent a visible flash of the wrong theme during startup when the runtime allows early preference restoration.

For React, a theme provider can apply the selected `data-theme` attribute, while Zustand stores the preference. In a Next.js application, make sure initial server rendering and client-side theme resolution do not produce hydration mismatches.

## 8. Advanced customization without breaking consistency

Professional users may want a different accent, denser panels, high contrast or a custom canvas appearance. Offer customization in controlled layers.

| Customization layer | What users may change                                                    |
| ------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------ |
| Preset theme        | Full semantic color palette                                              |
| Accent              | Primary action, focus and selection accents, with role-specific variants |
| Interface density   | Spacing, row height, control height                                      |
| Accessibility       | Contrast mode, text scaling, reduced motion                              |
| Canvas appearance   | Pasteboard, grid and guide colors                                        |
| Editor overlays     | Selection and diagnostic colors                                          |
| Visualization       | Heatmap and chart palettes                                               |
| Document appearance | Page background and artwork colors, stored separately                    |

Recommended priority order:

1. Mandatory application and accessibility constraints.
2. Explicit user accessibility preferences.
3. User-selected theme preset.
4. Validated custom semantic overrides.
5. Component-specific defaults.
6. Browser or operating-system defaults where appropriate.

This priority order should be implemented deliberately rather than relying on accidental CSS specificity. Some accessibility requirements may need to override custom colors when a custom configuration makes the interface unusable.

Respect `prefers-reduced-motion` for nonessential animations and transitions.&#x20;

